meta {
  name: System info
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/system/info
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{jwt_token}}
}

docs {
  Local host description + hardware inventory.

  Response shape:
    {
      "host_id": 1,
      "description": {
        "hostname": "...",
        "os": "...",
        "os_version": "...",
        "kernel": "...",
        "uptime_secs": 12345
      },
      "hardware": {
        "cpu_model": "...",
        "cpu_cores":   <physical>,
        "cpu_threads": <logical>,
        "total_memory_bytes": <u64>,
        "disks": [
          { "device_name", "mount_point", "fs_type", "total_bytes", "is_removable" }
        ],
        "network_interfaces": [
          { "name", "mac_address", "ip_addresses", "is_virtual" }
        ]
      }
    }

  Caching:
  - `description` is computed fresh on every call — `uptime_secs` is volatile,
    the other four fields are static but the underlying syscalls are cheap.
  - `hardware` is a snapshot taken once at server startup. Hot-plug events
    (USB disk, new NIC) require a server restart to be reflected. If the
    UI panel drives critical decisions off this data, surface a "captured at
    boot" hint near the disk / NIC list.

  Cross-platform notes:
  - `network_interfaces[].ip_addresses` is always `[]` today — sysinfo does
    not surface interface IPs. The field is in the shape so a later
    enrichment can land without breaking clients.
  - `is_virtual` is a name-prefix heuristic (`veth`, `docker`, `br-`); it
    will mark some bridges and miss others. Treat as "probably synthetic"
    rather than authoritative.
  - `cpu_cores` is the *physical* core count from sysinfo; on hyperthreaded
    Intel/AMD parts, `cpu_threads` will be higher.
  - On Windows, `os_version` and `kernel` strings come from the WMI bridge
    and can read awkwardly ("10.0.26100" vs "Windows 11 IoT Enterprise").
}
